      "nodes": [
        {
          "id": "domain-5-answer-monotonic",
          "title": "Binary-search a monotonic feasibility boundary",
          "concept_ids": [
            "dsa.binary.answer_space"
          ],
          "prerequisite_node_ids": [],
          "map_position": {
            "x": 0.16,
            "y": 0.5
          },
          "prompts": [
            {
              "id": "what",
              "kind": "what",
              "label": "WHAT MAKES ANSWER-SPACE SEARCH POSSIBLE?",
              "placeholder": "",
              "required": true,
              "reveal": {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "You do not need a sorted array. You need an ordered candidate answer domain and a predicate whose truth changes monotonically across that domain, such as `False, False, ..., True, True`.",
                "progressive_reveal": {
                  "spans": [
                    {
                      "id": "domain",
                      "text": "ordered candidate answer domain",
                      "required": true
                    },
                    {
                      "id": "predicate",
                      "text": "truth changes monotonically",
                      "required": true
                    }
                  ]
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "real-use",
              "kind": "connects_to",
              "label": "REAL USE: MINIMUM CAPACITY THAT MEETS AN SLA",
              "placeholder": "",
              "required": true,
              "reveal": {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Suppose `feasible(capacity)` simulates whether a workload can finish within an SLA. If every capacity above a feasible one is also feasible, search capacities for the smallest feasible value instead of testing every integer.",
                "progressive_reveal": {
                  "spans": [
                    {
                      "id": "sla",
                      "text": "smallest feasible value",
                      "required": true
                    }
                  ]
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "sequence",
              "kind": "mental_model",
              "label": "THE REASONING CHAIN",
              "placeholder": "",
              "required": true,
              "reveal": {
                "type": "sequence",
                "items": [
                  "Define the candidate answer range.",
                  "Write a feasibility predicate.",
                  "Prove the predicate is monotonic over that range.",
                  "Binary-search the false/true boundary.",
                  "Return the smallest feasible or largest infeasible value required by the contract."
                ]
              }
            }
          ],
          "source_refs": [
            {
              "title": "MIT OpenCourseWare 6.006 — Lecture 1: Algorithms and Computation",
              "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-006-introduction-to-algorithms-spring-2020/resources/lecture-1-algorithms-and-computation/"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "domain-5-answer-predicate",
          "title": "Prove the predicate before searching it",
          "concept_ids": [
            "dsa.binary.answer_space",
            "dsa.binary.boundary"
          ],
          "prerequisite_node_ids": [
            "domain-5-answer-monotonic"
          ],
          "map_position": {
            "x": 0.5,
            "y": 0.5
          },
          "prompts": [
            {
              "id": "what",
              "kind": "what",
              "label": "A BOOLEAN FUNCTION IS NOT ENOUGH",
              "placeholder": "",
              "required": true,
              "reveal": {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Binary search is valid only if the feasibility results form one transition across the ordered domain. If truth can flip back and forth, discarding half the answer space is not justified.",
                "progressive_reveal": {
                  "spans": [
                    {
                      "id": "transition",
                      "text": "one transition",
                      "required": true
                    },
                    {
                      "id": "flip",
                      "text": "flip back and forth",
                      "required": true
                    }
                  ]
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "real-use",
              "kind": "connects_to",
              "label": "REAL USE: CONCURRENCY LIMIT FOR A THROUGHPUT TARGET",
              "placeholder": "",
              "required": true,
              "reveal": {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "If increasing an idealized concurrency limit can never reduce whether a throughput target is achievable under the model, `meets_target(limit)` may be monotonic. If contention makes higher limits sometimes worse, that assumption fails and binary search needs rethinking.",
                "progressive_reveal": {
                  "spans": [
                    {
                      "id": "assumption",
                      "text": "If contention makes higher limits sometimes worse",
                      "required": true
                    }
                  ]
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "questions",
              "kind": "action",
              "label": "THREE THINGS TO WRITE DOWN",
              "placeholder": "",
              "required": true,
              "reveal": {
                "type": "bullets",
                "items": [
                  "What is the ordered candidate answer?",
                  "What exactly does `feasible(x)` mean?",
                  "Why does feasibility, once reached, stay reached (or once lost, stay lost)?"
                ]
              }
            }
          ],
          "source_refs": [
            {
              "title": "MIT OpenCourseWare 6.006 — Lecture 1: Algorithms and Computation",
              "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-006-introduction-to-algorithms-spring-2020/resources/lecture-1-algorithms-and-computation/"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "domain-5-answer-boundaries",
          "title": "Return the correct side of the feasibility boundary",
          "concept_ids": [
            "dsa.binary.answer_space",
            "dsa.binary.progress"
          ],
          "prerequisite_node_ids": [
            "domain-5-answer-predicate"
          ],
          "map_position": {
            "x": 0.82,
            "y": 0.5
          },
          "prompts": [
            {
              "id": "what",
              "kind": "what",
              "label": "MINIMUM FEASIBLE VS MAXIMUM INFEASIBLE",
              "placeholder": "",
              "required": true,
              "reveal": {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "A boundary search is only complete when you know which side the product/question wants. `False...True` can support 'first true' or 'last false'; those are adjacent but different answers.",
                "progressive_reveal": {
                  "spans": [
                    {
                      "id": "side",
                      "text": "which side",
                      "required": true
                    }
                  ]
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "real-use",
              "kind": "connects_to",
              "label": "REAL USE: SMALLEST WORKER COUNT THAT MEETS A DEADLINE",
              "placeholder": "",
              "required": true,
              "reveal": {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "If worker counts 1..W are ordered and `finishes_on_time(w)` is monotonic, the operational question might be the minimum worker count that passes. Returning the largest failing worker count would be off by one even though the search found the same transition.",
                "progressive_reveal": {
                  "spans": [
                    {
                      "id": "minimum",
                      "text": "minimum worker count that passes",
                      "required": true
                    }
                  ]
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "not-this",
              "kind": "not_this",
              "label": "DO NOT HIDE THE CONTRACT IN A TEMPLATE",
              "placeholder": "",
              "required": false,
              "reveal": {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Memorized binary-search templates are dangerous if you cannot state what the interval means and which boundary the loop is converging toward.",
                "progressive_reveal": {
                  "spans": [
                    {
                      "id": "meaning",
                      "text": "what the interval means",
                      "required": true
                    }
                  ]
                }
              }
            }
          ],
          "source_refs": [
            {
              "title": "MIT OpenCourseWare 6.006 — Lecture 1: Algorithms and Computation",
              "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-006-introduction-to-algorithms-spring-2020/resources/lecture-1-algorithms-and-computation/"
            },
            {
              "title": "Python documentation — bisect",
              "url": "https://docs.python.org/3/library/bisect.html"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
